86
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:54,340
A new study has found a positive correlation between how much television children

87
00:07:54,340 --> 00:07:56,940
watch and their parents' stress levels.

88
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:02,640
Why? Because the more television kids watch, the more they're exposed to

89
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:08,380
advertising. The more advertising they see, the more likely they are to insist on

90
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:11,700
purchasing items when they go with their parents to the store.

91
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:15,980
This could generate conflict if the parents refuse.

92
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:19,760
All that, researchers say, can contribute to parents'

93
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:23,340
stress levels. What's the solution?

94
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:27,620
Perhaps the most obvious is curtailing screen time.

95
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:32,780
Commercial content is there for a reason, to elicit purchasing behavior.

96
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:36,820
So, parents might want to shut off the TV.

97
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:42,600
Researchers concede that this is easier said than done, so they suggest another

98
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:47,520
option. Parents can change how they talk to their kids about purchases.

99
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:53,160
The researchers suggest that parents seek input from their children on family

100
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:54,300
purchasing decisions.

101
00:08:54,980 --> 00:08:57,400
They shouldn't try to control all purchases.

102
00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:03,480
Instead, parents might tell their children things like, I will listen to your advice

103
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:05,700
on certain products or brands.

104
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:11,160
This type of communication, the researchers assert, can lead to children making

105
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:12,880
fewer purchasing demands.

106
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:15,740
That means less parents' stress.

107
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:22,920
However, the protective effect of this kind of diminishes with greater exposure to

108
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:28,880
television. This is because advertising aimed at children is especially persuasive.

109
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:35,560
Advertisers use an assortment of tactics, such as bright colors, happy music, and

110
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:38,620
celebrity endorsements, to appeal to children.

111
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:44,920
Plus, children don't have the cognitive ability to fully understand advertising's

112
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:49,620
intent. That makes them particularly vulnerable to advertisements.

