117
00:10:49,260 --> 00:11:00,560
What TV Everyone is supposed to for good guys, and we should only punish the bad

118
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,620
guys. But that's not what we always do.

119
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,700
Most of the time, we do indeed reward good people.

120
00:11:08,180 --> 00:11:14,020
We also often punish people who harm others or who aren't good team players.

121
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:19,240
But sometimes, the good guys also get punished or criticized.

122
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:22,720
Specifically, because they are so good.

123
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:28,040
This seems baffling, because it's detrimental to group cooperation.

124
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:33,900
However, the phenomenon has been discovered in multiple fields, and it has been

125
00:11:33,900 --> 00:11:35,540
found in every society.

126
00:11:36,220 --> 00:11:37,100
Why does this happen?

127
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:39,840
Research suggests a simple reason.

128
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:44,840
When one person looks really good, others look bad by comparison.

129
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:50,120
Those others then have an incentive in stopping that person from looking good.

130
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:53,860
Especially if they can't or won't compete.

131
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:58,020
After all, we're all judged in comparison with others.

132
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:02,360
When faced with someone better, what can a normal person do?

133
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,440
One option is to actively compete.

134
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:08,860
A second option is to bring that person down.

135
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:14,780
That is, to suppress their cooperation or work ethic, infer selfish motives for

136
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:18,900
their actions, or imply real or imagined hypocrisy.

137
00:12:18,900 --> 00:12:25,520
Other tactics include attacking them on unrelated dimensions or punishing them

138
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:27,860
outright. Why does this matter?

139
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:32,520
often attack the motives of people who protect the environment,

140
00:12:33,180 --> 00:12:36,260
donate money, or work too hard.

141
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:42,620
Such good deeds are dismissed as naive or hypocritical by those who do not perform

142
00:12:42,620 --> 00:12:49,200
those deeds. This criticism may ultimately discourage people from doing good deeds.

143
00:12:49,960 --> 00:12:54,020
So it's important to recognize these attacks for what they are.

144
